   "parts": [
    [
     1,
     "<div dir=\"ltr\"><br><br>I response data (dichotomous) for a 47-item test with 11 distinct testlets. I am trying to see if a bifactor solution is a good fit for this data, but there is good reason to believe that the specific factors should all be correlated with one another. I have the following code: <div style=\"background-color:rgb(250,250,250);border-color:rgb(187,187,187);border-style:solid;border-width:1px\"><code><div><span style=\"color:#606\">Model</span><span style=\"color:#000\"> </span><span style=\"color:#660\">&lt;-</span><span style=\"color:#000\"> mirt</span><span style=\"color:#660\">.</span><span style=\"color:#000\">model</span><span style=\"color:#660\">(</span><span style=\"color:#080\">&#39;G = 1-47<br>                     COV = S1*S2*S3*S4*S5*S6*S7*S8*S9*S10*S11&#39;</span><span style=\"color:#660\">)</span><span style=\"color:#000\"><br><br></span><span style=\"color:#606\">Testlets</span><span style=\"color:#000\"> </span><span style=\"color:#660\">&lt;-</span><span style=\"color:#000\"> c</span><span style=\"color:#660\">(</span><span style=\"color:#000\">rep</span><span style=\"color:#660\">(</span><span style=\"color:#066\">1</span><span style=\"color:#660\">,</span><span style=\"color:#066\">7</span><span style=\"color:#660\">),</span><span style=\"color:#000\">rep</span><span style=\"color:#660\">(</span><span style=\"color:#066\">2</span><span style=\"color:#660\">,</span><span style=\"color:#066\">3</span><span style=\"color:#660\">),</span><span style=\"color:#000\">rep</span><span style=\"color:#660\">(</span><span style=\"color:#066\">3</span><span style=\"color:#660\">,</span><span style=\"color:#066\">3</span><span style=\"color:#660\">),</span><span style=\"color:#000\">rep</span><span style=\"color:#660\">(</span><span style=\"color:#066\">4</span><span style=\"color:#660\">,</span><span style=\"color:#066\">8</span><span style=\"color:#660\">),</span><span style=\"color:#000\">rep</span><span style=\"color:#660\">(</span><span style=\"color:#066\">5</span><span style=\"color:#660\">,</span><span style=\"color:#066\">5</span><span style=\"color:#660\">),</span><span style=\"color:#000\">rep</span><span style=\"color:#660\">(</span><span style=\"color:#066\">6</span><span style=\"color:#660\">,</span><span style=\"color:#066\">3</span><span style=\"color:#660\">),</span><span style=\"color:#000\">rep</span><span style=\"color:#660\">(</span><span style=\"color:#066\">7</span><span style=\"color:#660\">,</span><span style=\"color:#066\">5</span><span style=\"color:#660\">),</span><span style=\"color:#000\">rep</span><span style=\"color:#660\">(</span><span style=\"color:#066\">8</span><span style=\"color:#660\">,</span><span style=\"color:#066\">4</span><span style=\"color:#660\">),</span><font color=\"#000000\"><span style=\"color:#066\">9</span><span style=\"color:#660\">,</span><span style=\"color:#000\">rep</span><span style=\"color:#660\">(</span><span style=\"color:#066\">10</span><span style=\"color:#660\">,</span><span style=\"color:#066\">4</span><span style=\"color:#660\">),</span><span style=\"color:#000\">rep</span><span style=\"color:#660\">(</span><span style=\"color:#066\">11</span><span style=\"color:#660\">,</span><span style=\"color:#066\">4</span><span style=\"color:#660\">))</span><span style=\"color:#000\"><br><br></span></font><span style=\"color:#000\">bi1 </span><span style=\"color:#660\">&lt;-</span><span style=\"color:#000\"> bfactor</span><span style=\"color:#660\">(</span><span style=\"color:#606\">ItemDat</span><span style=\"color:#660\">,</span><span style=\"color:#606\">Testlets</span><span style=\"color:#660\">,</span><span style=\"color:#606\">Model</span><span style=\"color:#660\">,</span><span style=\"color:#000\"> TOL </span><span style=\"color:#660\">=</span><span style=\"color:#000\"> </span><span style=\"color:#066\">1e-3</span><span style=\"color:#660\">)</span><span style=\"color:#000\"><br><br><br></span></div></code></div><br><div>When I look at the model, the estimated covariance between all specific factors in a pairwise fashion is equal to 0.25. Is there some underlying assumption or snytax that I am missing? This doesn&#39;t make any sense.</div></div>"
    ]
