  {
   "id": "k1c4phAUGwAJ",
   "from": "Phil Chalmers",
   "author": "b330c2028fed415b",
   "date": "2019-02-12T17:25:26Z",
   "subject": "Re: Covariance estimation in bifactor model?",
   "parts": [
    [
     1,
     "<div dir=\"ltr\">The bfactor() function imposes a orthogonal structure on the specific factors. Hence, providing covariance parameters other than 0 will just be ignored (this should probably throw an error, but it is documented in the function nonetheless). <div><br></div><div>That said, would this model even be identified? The global covariation between the specific factors would be absorbed by the G factor. At best I think you can correlate some of the specific factors in a bifactor structure, but not all of them. And in this case, the bfactor() function wouldn&#39;t be appropriate, so you couldn&#39;t estimate the model using the dimension-reduction algorithms provided. <div><br clear=\"all\"><div><div dir=\"ltr\" class=\"gmail_signature\" data-smartmail=\"gmail_signature\"><div dir=\"ltr\"><div>Phil</div></div></div></div><br></div></div></div><br><div class=\"gmail_quote\"></div>"
    ],
    [
     3,
     "<div class=\"gmail_quote\"><div dir=\"ltr\" class=\"gmail_attr\">On Tue, Feb 5, 2019 at 9:24 PM Eric Burkholder &lt;<a href data-email-masked rel=\"nofollow\">eric1...@gmail.com</a>&gt; wrote:<br></div></div>"
    ],
    [
